# infra/supabase/migrator/.env.example — `migrator` container surface.
#
# ADR-072 (Option E) production migration-apply mechanism. DevOps-owned.
# Per-surface secret confinement (same convention as workers/etl/.env.example,
# workers/provider-sync/.env.example, workers/pdf-render/.env.example,
# root .env.example): this file enumerates ONLY what THIS container is
# permitted to hold — the enumeration IS the confinement property.
#
# ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-09-18, ADR-072 Amendment 4 / BACKLOG.md §7.36 item 29:
# "the enumeration IS the confinement property" line above needs the same
# re-grading docs/deployment-runbook.md §5 records for every per-surface
# .env.example — it is a claim about what THIS FILE says, not about what
# Coolify actually injects into a multi-service application (ADR-072
# Amendment 3 measured that gap). Since `migrator` moved to its own
# ONE-SERVICE Coolify application (infra/supabase/migrator/docker-compose.yaml),
# it is no longer inside a multi-service application at all — Amendment 3's
# `env_file:`-to-every-sibling mechanism has no siblings left to leak to,
# so this file's enumeration is confinement-by-construction again, not
# merely confinement-by-declaration. This has NOT been independently
# measured against a one-service Coolify application (Amendment 3 measured
# the multi-service case only) — see provision-migrator-app.sh's own
# "Verification" step, which checks this by `docker inspect` on the new
# container rather than assuming it from this file's enumeration.
#
# Anchors: ADR-072 Decision 1 (mechanism) / Decision 4 (credential model,
# Sec C7/C8) / Amendment 4 (topology — standalone application) ·
# secrets-manifest.yml (`production_only`) ·
# infra/supabase/migrator/docker-compose.yaml (`migrator` service —
# PROD_DB_URL is ASSEMBLED there from the two vars below; this container
# never receives a pre-built connection string as a literal secret).

# ============================================================================
# DB LOGIN ROLE = `migrator`   (ADR-072 Decision 4 — a BOUNDED dedicated role:
# CREATEROLE + owner of the app database. NOT `postgres`, NOT `authenticator`,
# NOT any `pfin_*` worker role, and NOT a member of `service_role` /
# `authenticated` — Sec C7/C8.)
# ============================================================================
# Non-secret username. Distinct from every other login identity in this
# stack (`postgres`, `authenticator`, `pfin_etl`, `pfin_provider_sync`) —
# see docker-compose.yml's migrator service comment for the full rationale.
MIGRATOR_DB_USER=migrator

# secret (production_only, secrets-manifest.yml) — the `migrator` role's own
# password. Confined to THIS container's env only: never CI, never the
# `app` container, never any other worker. Generate with `openssl rand -hex
# 32` (256-bit), matching this repo's other role-credential convention
# (pfin_etl / pfin_provider_sync — see docs/deployment-runbook.md §6.1/§6.2).
#
# ⚠ BOOTSTRAPPING — ANSWERED (was an open question at the chunk-1 PR;
# resolved by ADR-072 Amendment 5, 2026-09-16, "ownership BY CONSTRUCTION"):
# the `migrator` role IS created by a migration file (`118_migrator_role.sql`),
# applied under the FIRST supervised bootstrap pre-step (§6.3 Phase 1, as
# `supabase_admin`) — never by a separate hand-run operator step, and never
# by `migrator` itself (it cannot create the role it needs to exist first).
# `migrator`'s password is set and LOGIN flipped in that same supervised
# pass, per docs/deployment-runbook.md §6.3/§6.5. This paragraph is left
# in place, corrected rather than deleted, as the record of what was open
# at chunk 1 and how it was closed — see DECISIONS.md's ADR-072 Amendment 5
# for the full ruling.
MIGRATOR_DB_PASSWORD=
